=== How to use ===
1. Wire the plugin into a declarative agent through actions and package it with the app manifest.
2. Make the API enforce the real rules: the caller may only decide on requests that are assigned to them, and decisions are idempotent (return 409 when the request was already decided).
3. Write the confirmation body so that it says what happens next, for example that the requester is notified.
4. Test three cases: ambiguous request (several pending), unknown request ID, and a request that someone else already decided.
5. If the same decision should also appear in Microsoft Teams Approvals or Power Automate, call the existing flow from your API and keep this plugin as the thin front door.

=== Common mistakes ===
- Setting isNonConsequential to true for write operations; it lets users pick Always Allow, which removes the per-decision safety net.
- Omitting the reason parameter for rejections, which leads to empty rejections.
- Letting the model pass a requestId from memory. Force the list-first order in the reasoning state.
- Declaring only GetPrivateData for write functions. Use ResourceStateUpdate so that the plugin is classified correctly.
- Using one generic confirmation text for both decisions; users then cannot tell approve and reject apart.

