=== How to use ===
1. Create the API key registration in the Teams developer portal (Tools, API key registration) for the base URL kb.contoso.example and copy the registration ID into the manifest.
2. Add icons named outline.png (32x32, transparent, white outline) and color.png (192x192) next to the three JSON files and zip all files without a folder level.
3. Upload the package with Apps, Manage your apps, Upload an app, then use the app from the compose box or by typing its name in the command box.
4. Test the API response in a REST client first, then preview the card JSON with sample data in the Adaptive Card designer.
5. Return localized text if you can: Teams sends an accept-language header based on the user's locale.

=== Common mistakes ===
- Naming the parameter differently in the manifest and in OpenAPI; the request cannot be built then.
- Adding more than one required parameter without a default, or arrays and oneOf, anyOf, allOf or not constructs in the request, which are not supported.
- Using the wrong value for type. Command types are query and action; there is no search type.
- Forgetting validDomains for the domain of card links and images.
- Expecting the extension to show up in Microsoft 365 Copilot. It is a Teams feature; for Copilot build an API plugin.

A bot-based action message extension. Action commands open a dialog and submit its values to your bot. This sample defines two commands in one extension (Teams supports one message extension per app, with up to 10 commands): createTaskFromMessage appears in the more actions menu of a message and sets fetchTask to true so that the bot builds the dialog dynamically, and createTaskManual shows a static form that Teams renders from the parameters array. The bot must have the same botId as the extension. Manifest version 1.30 from Microsoft Learn.

